.TH SED 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
sed \- filter and transform text using a script of editing commands
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sed
.RB [ \-n ]
.I script
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B sed
.RB [ \-n ]
.RI [ \-e " script" ]\ .\|.\|.
.RI [ \-f " script_file" ]\ .\|.\|.
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sed
reads each
.I file
operand (or standard input, if none are given, or if a
.I file
operand is exactly
.BR \- )
fully into memory, then repeatedly applies the assembled editing
script to it one input line at a time (a "cycle"), writing the result
of each cycle to standard output as it goes; line numbers used by
addresses count cumulatively across every
.I file
operand, as if all of them were one input.
.PP
Unless
.B \-n
is given, the pattern space is written to standard output at the end
of every cycle that was not deleted; a leading "#n" as the first two
bytes of the assembled script has the same effect as
.BR \-n .
.PP
If neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
is given at all, the first operand is
.IR script ,
and every remaining operand is a
.IR file .
Otherwise every operand is a
.IR file ,
and the script is the concatenation of every
.B \-e
and
.B \-f
argument, in the order their options appear on the command line; a
newline is inserted between two consecutive pieces only when the
first of the two came from
.BR \-e .
.SH REGULAR EXPRESSIONS
Every regular expression in a script (an address, or the left half of
.BR s /// )
is a basic regular expression; there is no extended-regular-expression
mode. An empty regular expression, written
.B //
(or, with a custom delimiter, that same delimiter used twice in a row
right after the backslash)
reuses whichever regular expression was most recently
.I used
by any address or
.BR s /// ,
not necessarily the one written immediately before it in the script.
.SH ADDRESSES
Any command may be preceded by zero, one, or two addresses.
.TP
no address
The command applies to every cycle.
.TP
one address
The command applies only to the cycle(s) the address selects.
.TP
.I addr1\c
.BR , \c
.I addr2
The command applies to a range of lines: starting at the first line
.I addr1
selects, through the next line at or after it that
.I addr2
selects (or through the last line of input, if
.I addr2
never subsequently matches). If
.I addr2
is a line number less than or equal to the line number that opened the
range, the range is exactly that one line. Once a range has closed,
.I addr1
is searched for again starting at the following line; it is never
re-triggered while its own range is still open.
.PP
Each address is one of:
.TP
.I number
A decimal line number.
.TP
.B $
The last line of input.
.TP
.IB / bre /
or
.IB \e c bre c
A context address (any byte
.I c
other than backslash or newline may be used as the delimiter in the
second form): selected on any cycle whose pattern space the basic
regular expression
.I bre
matches.
.PP
Any address (including the second address of a range) may be followed
by
.B !
to select the opposite of what it would otherwise select.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.IB [ addr ]\c
.BR a \e
.I text
Queue
.I text
for output; see OUTPUT TIMING below. At most one address may be given.
.TP
.IB [ addr ]\c
.BR i \e
.I text
Write
.I text
to standard output immediately. At most one address may be given.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR c \e
.I text
Delete the addressed lines from the pattern space and write
.I text
in their place (once, when a two-address range closes, not once per
line in the range).
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR d
Delete the pattern space and start the next cycle without writing it.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR D
If the pattern space contains a newline, delete everything through the
first one and restart the current cycle's commands from the beginning
with the remainder, without reading a new line of input; otherwise,
the same as
.BR d .
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR g
Replace the pattern space with a copy of the hold space.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR G
Append a newline and a copy of the hold space to the pattern space.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR h
Replace the hold space with a copy of the pattern space.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR H
Append a newline and a copy of the pattern space to the hold space.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR x
Exchange the pattern space and the hold space.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR n
Unless
.B \-n
is in effect, write the pattern space to standard output; then replace
it with the next line of input. If there is no next line, quit without
starting a new cycle (this happens only after the write above has
already run).
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR N
Append a newline and the next line of input to the pattern space. If
there is no next line, quit without starting a new cycle and without
any output at all for this incomplete final cycle.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR p
Write the pattern space to standard output.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR P
Write the pattern space to standard output, up through its first
embedded newline (or all of it, if it has none).
.TP
.IB [ addr ]\c
.BR q
Finish the current cycle's normal end-of-cycle output (unless
.BR \-n )
then quit. At most one address may be given.
.TP
.IB [ addr ]\c
.B =
Write the current cumulative line number to standard output. At most
one address may be given.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR l
Write the pattern space to standard output in a visually unambiguous
form: non-printable and non-ASCII bytes are written as backslash
escapes, long lines are folded at a fixed width of 70 columns, and the
result is terminated with an unescaped
.BR $ .
.TP
.IB [ addr ]\c
.BR r " file"
Queue the contents of
.I file
for output; see OUTPUT TIMING below.
.I file
is read fresh at the moment it is actually written, not when
.B r
itself runs; a
.I file
that is missing or cannot be read is treated as empty. At most one
address may be given.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR w " file"
Append the pattern space, followed by a newline, to
.IR file .
.I file
is opened (and truncated) at most once per distinct name for the
whole run, no matter how many times
.B w
writes to it. The names
.B /dev/stdout
and
.B /dev/stderr
are recognized specially and write to
.BR sed 's
own standard output or standard error instead of a real file of that
name.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR s / bre / replacement / flags
Substitute
.I replacement
for text matching
.I bre
in the pattern space. Any byte other than backslash or newline may be
used as the delimiter in place of
.BR / ,
consistently, in all three fields.
.I flags
is zero or more of: a decimal occurrence number (default 1, selecting
which match to act on);
.B g
(also replace every match from the numbered occurrence onward, instead
of only that one);
.B p
(write the pattern space if a substitution was actually made); and
.BI w\  file
(append the pattern space to
.I file ,
the same way the
.B w
command does, if a substitution was actually made \(em this consumes
the rest of the line, so it must be the last flag given).
.I replacement
may contain
.B &
(the whole matched text) and
.BI \e n
for
.I n
1\(en9 (the text matched by the
.IR n th
parenthesized subexpression of
.IR bre ).
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BR y / string1 / string2 /
Transliterate: replace every byte in the pattern space that equals a
byte of
.I string1
with the byte at the same position in
.IR string2 .
Any byte other than backslash or newline may be used as the delimiter
in place of
.BR / .
.I string1
and
.I string2
must be the same length after their own backslash escapes are
resolved.
.TP
.BI : label
Define
.I label
as a branch target for
.B b
and
.BR t .
May not be preceded by an address.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BI b\  label
Branch to the command following
.BI : label
(or, if
.I label
is omitted, to the end of the script), starting the next command from
there instead of falling through in script order.
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.BI t\  label
Like
.BR b ,
but branches only if at least one successful substitution has
happened since the last line was read or the last
.B t
branched; taking the branch clears that condition.
.TP
.B #
A comment: the rest of the line is ignored. (Only meaningful as the
very first thing in the script does "#n" have the additional effect
described under DESCRIPTION.)
.TP
.IB [ addr1 , addr2 ]\c
.B {
.I commands
.B }
Groups
.I commands
under one address or address range, exactly as if each of them had
that same address or range of its own.
.SH OUTPUT TIMING
Text queued by
.B a
or
.B r
is written to standard output just before the next attempt to read a
line of input during
.B N
or
.BR n ,
or when the script reaches its own end for the current cycle \(em
never at the point where
.B a
or
.B r
itself runs. Entries queue in the order they were applied and are
written in that same order.
.B i
is not queued at all; it always writes immediately.
.SH TRAILING NEWLINE
.B sed
does not track whether the very last line of the very last
.I file
operand originally lacked a trailing newline: every line read has any
trailing newline of its own stripped before it reaches the script, and
every line
.B sed
writes gets exactly one newline added back, regardless of what the
original input had.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Suppress the automatic end-of-cycle write of the pattern space to
standard output; only explicit output commands
.RB ( p ,
.BR P ,
.BR l ,
and
.BR s /// 's
.B p
flag) produce output.
.TP
.BI \-e " script"
Append
.I script
to the editing script (see DESCRIPTION for how
.BR \-e ,
.BR \-f ,
and a bare
.I script
operand interact). May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-f " script_file"
Append the contents of
.I script_file
to the editing script.
.I script_file
may be
.B \-
for standard input. May be given more than once.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I script
The editing script, used only when neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
was given.
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file, or
.B \-
for standard input. If none are given, standard input is read.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
The following are not implemented, and are refused with a diagnostic
and a nonzero exit status rather than being silently approximated:
in-place editing;
an extended-regular-expression mode;
a
.B Q
command (immediate quit with no final automatic write);
an exit-code operand on
.B q
or
.BR Q ;
case-insensitive or multiline
.RB ( i / I ,
.BR m / M )
flags on
.BR s /// ;
.BR \eU / \eL / \eE
case-conversion escapes in a
.B s///
replacement;
the
.BR 0 , /re/
address form;
an explicit line-wrap-length operand on
.BR l ,
which is always 70 columns here; and the one-line
.IB a\  text\c
,
.IB i\  text\c
,
.IB c\  text
shorthand some other implementations accept without the backslash \(em
.BR a \e ,
.BR i \e ,
and
.B c\e
here always require the backslash-then-newline form described above,
or text directly on the same line right after the backslash.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every line of input was processed successfully.
.TP
.B >0
An error occurred: invalid usage, an invalid option, a script that
could not be parsed (an unknown command, an unterminated expression,
unbalanced
.B {
and
.BR } ,
a branch to an undefined label, an invalid regular expression, or
.BR y /// 's
two strings differing in length), a
.I file
that could not be opened or read, an empty regular expression used
before any regular expression had yet been used at all, or a failure
writing output.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR grep (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR expr (1)
